{"_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Decision Support System", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 12, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 2, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "A2.1", "type": "level"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"content": "These are words you hear and use to talk about Decision Support System and technology.", "type": "text"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Describe common activities at work to a visitor using short sentences in familiar spoken and written situations.  Understand information about data and its structure in simple spoken and written situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Identifies the expressions and vocabulary necessary for the description of places of work and jobs in common written and spoken situations.   Describes common activities related to Software Programming through the use of appropriate expressions, grammar and vocabulary in written or spoken situations according to the abilities related to this level.", "type": "secondary_1"}], "type": "objectives"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le02ob12re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Review of Simple Present, Present Continuous, Be Going to in Affirmative, Negative and Questions", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "There are some very important tenses that we have to use in our daily life. Let's review some of them. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "A Decision Support System is a computer application, this application provides access to fundamental information.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "<span class=\"bullet\"><strong>Simple Present:</strong><br></br> We use the<b> simple present</b> to talk about permanent habitual facts in the present. Let's remember the structure of the <b>simple present</b>:</br></br><center>  Subject (person animal or thing that performs an action) + verb (action performed)* + complement (object of the action, time, or/and place).<br><br></center>*Bear in mind that when you have a third person subject, the verb you use changes adding a final <b>\"-s\"</b>, <b>\"-es\"</b>, or <b>\"-ies\"</b>. </br></br>  Examples: <br> <span class=\"italic\">We <b>work</b> on the design of apps for computers.<br/>  She <b>develops</b> applications.</span><br><br>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "For the negative and interrogative forms of the <b>simple present</b> tense,  use the auxiliary verb <strong>\u201cdo\u201d</strong> or <b>\"does\"</b> depending on the subject.  Remember that whenever we use auxiliaries, the main verb does not change.<br><br>  Examples:<br> <span class=\"italic\"><strong>Do</strong> you like these apps? <br/>She <strong>doesn't</strong> understand what DSS is</span>.<br><br>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"bullet\"><strong>Present Continuous:</strong><br><br>We use the <b>present continuous</b> to talk about actions that are happening at the moment of speaking, or/and temporary situations.  The structure of the <b>present continuous</b> has the verb <strong>\u201cbe\u201d</strong> and an action verb with <strong>\u201cing\u201d</strong> at the end. To make the negative statement, we just have to add the particle <strong>\u201cnot\u201d</strong> next to the verb <strong>\u201cbe\u201d</strong>. Don't forget to conjugate the verb \"be\". Plus, you can be precise about the actual moment the event takes place. </br></br> Examples:<br> <span class=\"italic\">I<b>'m working</b> on my apps <strong>at the moment.<br>  This year we're developing </strong> an amazing software system.</span><br>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "To make questions in the <b>present continuous</b>, we place the verb <strong>\u201cbe\u201d</strong> (conjugated) before the subject. Just as in the simple present, only a <strong>-wh</strong> word can come before the verb <strong>\u201cbe\u201d</strong>.<br><br>  Examples:<br> <span class=\"italic\"><strong>Are</strong> we <b>working</b> on the project now? <br/><strong>What</strong> colors <b>are</b> we <b>using</b> on this app?</span><br><br>  ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "We can also use the <b>present continuous</b> to express near future plans. Remember this is only for near future, for long term plans we use the going to. <br><br>  Examples:<br>  <span class=\"italic\">We<b>'re finishing</b> the first app <strong>tomorrow.</strong><br> We<b>'re</b> not <b>working</b> next <strong>Monday.</strong><br><br>  <span class=\"bullet\"><strong>Future with going to:</strong></br><br> We use the <strong>be going to</strong> structure to talk about plans in the future.<br><br>  Examples:<br> <span class=\"italic\"><b>I'm going to design</b> an app next week.<br> <b>We are going to visit</b> the client to collect requirements next month. </span><br><br>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "The structure of the <strong>be going to</strong> is quite similar to the one we use with the present continuous, with the difference that the -<strong>ing</strong> verb is always going to be <strong>going to </strong> to introduce an infinitive verb. To make the negative and interrogative forms, we add <strong>not</strong> to the verb <strong>be</strong> conjugated; and for questions, we put the verb <strong>be</strong> before the subject, just as we do in the present continuous. <br><br>  Examples:<br> <span class=\"italic\">We <strong>are not</strong> <b>going to continue</b> with that project.<br/>  Why <strong>are you</strong> <b>going to quit</b> the app project?</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Are you following the time line? ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "We are going to give a careful look at this topic.", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Review of Modal Verbs Must-Have to, Can - Could, in Affirmative, Negative and Questions", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "<strong>Modal verbs</strong> are auxiliary verbs that cannot work as the main verb in a sentence. They are used to express possibility, obligation, advisability, request, necessity and ability.   ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "How much data can a Decision Support System store?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "We should think of a DSS as one or more large databases.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "All these elements must help the user explore different alternatives if the DSS is well designed.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "<strong>Grammar structure</strong><br> <b>Modal verbs</b> cannot be the main verb of a sentence. They go along with it to express any of the functions stated above in the definition section. To use <b>modal verbs</b>, follow this structure:<br><br><center>  Subject + modal + verb (bare form) + complement<br><br></center>  Example:<br> <span class=\"italic\">I think we <strong>cannot continue</strong> using these computers. The whole system is getting very slow.</span><br><br><br>   <strong>Advisability</strong><br> It refers to the action of giving advice, recommendation and suggestions to those who need it. The <b>modal verbs</b> that are used for this are:<br><ul> <li>should</li> <li>must</li> <li>could</li> <li>can</li> <li>had better</li></ul>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Examples:<br> <span class=\"italic\">To speed up the process of software design, you <strong>should implement</strong> technologies with easy access.<br>If you are really interested in software design, you <strong>must use</strong> this app.</span><br><br><strong>Probability</strong><br/> Certainty and uncertainty have a lot to do with probability. Depending on how certain we feel about someone or something, we may use any of the <b>modal verbs</b> outlined below:<ul><li>will         (certain)</li> <li>could     (less certain)</li> <li>may       (fairly more uncertain)</li> <li>might     (the most uncertain)</li></ul>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Examples:<br> <span class=\"italic\">Sahra sent me an e-mail. She says she<strong> will be</strong> in the meeting today.<br>  He said he has too much office work. In case he can solve some urgent issues, he <strong>might attend</strong> the meeting.<br><br></span>  <strong>Obligation</strong> <br/>Companies, schools, colleges and other conglomerate institutions have rules and procedures that employees have to obey. To achieve goals we must also enforce ourselves with discipline and constancy. The <b>modal verbs</b> we use to express obligation are:<br><ul><li>have to         (external imposition)</li> <li>have got to   (external imposition)</li> <li>must             (personal imposition)</li></ul>  Examples:</br> <span class=\"italic\">We <strong>have to enter</strong> a double password to get access to any project content of the company.<br>I <strong>must use</strong> the security password if I want to enter any content.</span><br><br>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<strong>Ability</strong><br/> In order to state what we are capable of  doing with our personal skills, knowledge, experience and talents, we use:<br><ul><li>can                      (present abilities)</li> <li>could                   (past abilities)</li> <li>will be able to      (future abilities)</li></ul> Examples:<br> <span class=\"italic\">For some time I <strong>could write</strong> code, but I've forgotten. <br>I <strong>can make</strong> companies save money by designing an app.</span>", "type": "text"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "analytical ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "using analysis ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: In the 60's, scientists started a more systematic and analytical research on DSS. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le02ob12aud01", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "management ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the process of managing something", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Data management is a very important aspect when talking about investments and projects.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le02ob12aud02", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "app", "type": "name"}, {"content": "short form of \u201capplication\u201d. The use given to a computer program", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: A DSS is a very useful computer app that helps analytic and decision making processes in companies.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le02ob12aud03", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "symbiosis ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a relationship  that involves dependence between two elements or entities", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Research included the idea of \u201chuman-computer symbiosis\u201d for problem solving.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le02ob12aud04", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "transaction ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "something transacted; a business agreement", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: We should think of a DSS as one or more large databases that might be stored from transactions.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le02ob12aud05", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": " systematic ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "using a system or method", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Scientists started a more systematic and analytical research.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le02ob12aud06", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Go online and find additional information about the use DSS in business, and some specific DSS software packages.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Select the most important information, including advantages of using DSS for companies", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Create a brochure in a piece of paper, in which you present the information you found. Remember to use modal verbs, simple and continuous structures to talk about DSS, its applications and advantages.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Present the brochure to your peers, pretending you're selling them DSS as a product.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"content": "I research about the business applications of Decision Support Systems.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I collect relevant information on the subject of DSS in written form.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I share my findings with my peers.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"exercises": [{"questions": [], "template_override": 1, "stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": " I can read about the Decision Support System.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " I can write meaningful sentences related to the Decision Support System.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " I can explain important facts related to the Decision Support System with correct sentences.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " What do you think a support system is?", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": " Why is it important to express facts on technology in a written manner?", "type": "Free Header Item"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}], "type": "group"}, {"content": {"incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "try again", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time", "type": "text"}], "allowed_attempts": 2, "correct_feedback": "Correct", "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "You have 2 tries", "type": "text"}, {"content": "you have 1 attempt left", "type": "text"}]}, "type": "feedback"}, {"content": "Ask